{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Why\n\n`[operator-attested]` (ADR-0043) is the acceptance-criteria qualifier that tells the completion\nverifier \"this criterion's done-evidence inherently lives OUTSIDE the codebase, so accept a\nrecorded attestation instead of hunting for code proof\".\n\nThe word `operator` mis-signals. Across this repo it means a *human specifically* — \"the\noperator runbook\", \"the operator guide\", \"the operator escape hatch\", \"the operator can\" — and\nthe detector even carries a marker family named `human`. Agents therefore read\n`[operator-attested]` as \"a person must do this\", when in fact an agent recording a Gerrit vote\noutcome or a live-run result attests exactly as well. The tag names the wrong axis: WHO attests,\nrather than WHERE the evidence lives.\n\nRename the canonical tag to `[non-codebase]`, the missing half of a pair the vocabulary already\nhas. `[codebase]` is ALREADY a recognized tag (ADR-0043 §exact-match; `completion_verifier.md:95`;\n`plan_review_evidence_kind.md:56`; used on seven ACs of ticket 7226 in the corpus). It is\nprompt-level only — no regex parses it — but it is established author vocabulary, so\n`[non-codebase]` is not a coinage.\n\nThe new name also RESISTS the attestation-laundering anti-pattern by construction. To tag a\ncriterion whose proof is `tests/unit/test_scan_scoping.py` (the live bug 2f56 case), an author\nmust assert that file is \"non-codebase\" — self-evidently false. The name does the\n`det_attestation_launder` guard's work in the author's head before the guard ever runs.\n\n`[operator-attested]` is RETAINED as an accepted but UNDOCUMENTED compatibility alias. This is\nnot optional: 827 of 4,285 tickets in the live store (19%) carry the tag. Those are immutable\nevent-sourced records; a hard cutover would silently reclassify every one of their attested ACs\nas codebase-verifiable and break their close path.\n\n## Scope decisions (operator-approved)\n\n- The completion-verdict `kind` wire value `\"operator-attested\"` -> `\"non-codebase\"` IS in scope.\n  Blast radius is ONE comparison site (`src/rebar/audit/page.py:329`); the schema declares `kind`\n  as unconstrained `\"type\": \"string\"` with no enum. Sequenced reader-widen BEFORE emitter-change\n  so legacy persisted verdicts keep rendering.\n- Python identifiers are NOT renamed: the `det_operator_attested` module,\n  `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_TAG_RE`, `_OPERATOR_ATTESTED_AC_RE`, the `operator_attested` finding\n  attribute, the `operator_attested_gaps` coverage key. This keeps the object-identity re-export\n  seam and its tests untouched, and removes 5 files from the change set entirely\n  (`det_citation.py`, `det_clarity.py`, `test_det_citation.py`, `test_decide_ops_seam.py`,\n  `test_plan_review_portability.py` reference only the symbols, never the tag string).\n\n## What makes this tractable\n\nTWO deterministic matchers must be widened, not one: the shared Python regex, AND the separate\n`.rebar/criteria_routing.json` `text_all` activation trigger that decides whether\n`project.measurement-provenance` fires at all (a trigger not widened does not error — it\nSILENTLY skips the criterion).\n\nThe `kind` wire-value rename has a READER site and EMITTER sites, and they are sequenced apart:\n`src/rebar/audit/page.py:329` is the only READER (the sole comparison in the codebase); the\nEMITTERS are `src/rebar/llm/contracts.py:116`, `src/rebar/schemas/completion_verdict.schema.json`,\nand `src/rebar/llm/reviewers/completion_verifier.md`. Reader-widen lands first (S2) so the 827\nlive tickets holding legacy verdicts keep rendering when the emitter changes (S7).\n\nAll four deterministic consumers share the IDENTICAL compiled regex object — verified: `decide_ops`,\n`workflow_ops`, `det_attestation_launder`, and `det_measurement_provenance` each satisfy `is`\nagainst `det_operator_attested._OPERATOR_ATTESTED_TAG_RE`. Widening that ONE pattern propagates\neverywhere with no other code change, and needs no new branch anywhere — which is why\n`det_floor.py` (793 of its 800-line hard cap) and `decide_ops.plan_review_decide` (complexity 21,\ngrandfathered at threshold 15) require no edits and neither gate fires.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The canonical tag matcher accepts `[non-codebase]` and `[operator-attested]`\n      case-insensitively, rejects the `[operator_attested]` near-miss, and uses a NON-capturing\n      alternation so `findall()` arity is unchanged — proved by a unit test asserting\n      `operator_attested_ac_texts` returns a list of `str`, not tuples.\n- [ ] Every existing test that feeds `[operator-attested]` passes UNCHANGED, demonstrating the\n      alias still works.\n- [ ] `[non-codebase]` and `[operator-attested]` produce identical outcomes across the plan-time\n      lint, the laundering guard, the provenance guard, the AC-checkbox precheck, and the close\n      path — proved by parametrizing those suites over both spellings.\n- [ ] `project.measurement-provenance` fires on a `[non-codebase]`-tagged plan (the\n      `.rebar/criteria_routing.json` trigger was widened), proved by a test asserting the trigger\n      matches both spellings.\n- [ ] Author-facing guidance emits `[non-codebase]` only; `[operator-attested]` appears in NO\n      documented surface, enforced by a NEGATIVE test over the packaged guide.\n- [ ] `src/rebar/audit/page.py` renders a legacy `kind: \"operator-attested\"` verdict identically\n      to a new `kind: \"non-codebase\"` one, proved by a test over both fixture shapes.\n- [ ] The recognition-side prompts (`completion_verifier.md`, `plan_review_evidence_kind.md`,\n      `plan_review_ac_process_gate.md`, `.rebar/prompts/plan-review-project-measurement-provenance.md`)\n      instruct that BOTH spellings are recognized, so the LLM classification agrees with the\n      deterministic matcher.\n- [ ] The EMITTED classification vocabulary is cut over in lockstep with the tag, so the\n      deterministic matcher and the LLM's emitted `kind` cannot diverge: the verifier emits\n      `kind: \"non-codebase\"` (`completion_verifier.md:224`), `contracts.py:116` and\n      `completion_verdict.schema.json` declare it, and the `acceptance_items_classified`\n      checklist vocabulary at `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json:1241` names it.\n      That file is edited for BOTH its `text_all` trigger and its classification strings.\n- [ ] A new ADR (0101) amends ADR-0043 to record the rename, why `operator` mis-signalled, why\n      `attested` alone was rejected, and that `[operator-attested]` is retained as a deliberately\n      UNDOCUMENTED alias. ADR-0043 itself is unmodified; the generated ADR index is regenerated.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, and the generated criteria guide is regenerated with\n      its parity check and contract digests updated in the same commit."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787187317908161001,"uuid":"081a48d4-9780-44c1-803d-cf25596a0002"}